WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.060
FedRAMP stands for the Federal Risk and Authorization Management Program.

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:07.780
It is a United States government program that standardizes security assessment,

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:12.250
authorization, and continuous monitoring for cloud services used by federal agencies.

00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:14.290
The basic purpose is consistency.

00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:17.860
Before FedRAMP, agencies could evaluate cloud services in different ways,

00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:20.740
which created duplicated work and uneven expectations.

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:23.460
FedRAMP gives agencies, cloud providers, assessors,

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:26.105
and stakeholders a shared process and vocabulary.

00:00:26.105 --> 00:00:30.195
FedRAMP matters because federal agencies rely on cloud services to support mission and

00:00:30.195 --> 00:00:31.545
business functions.

00:00:31.545 --> 00:00:34.870
Those services may process, store, or transmit government information,

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:38.920
so agencies need a structured way to understand and manage cloud security risk.

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:41.685
FedRAMP is not simply a form or a product label.

00:00:41.685 --> 00:00:45.140
It combines security controls, documentation, independent assessment,

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:46.440
authorization decisions,

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:50.240
and ongoing monitoring into a repeatable program for federal cloud risk management.

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:54.790
A cloud service provider may use FedRAMP to demonstrate that its cloud offering has been

00:00:54.790 --> 00:00:57.730
assessed against common federal security expectations.

00:00:57.730 --> 00:01:00.940
Agencies can then use that information as part of their own risk decisions.

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:06.230
The program is built on NIST guidance, especially NIST SP 800-53 security controls.

00:01:06.230 --> 00:01:09.990
That connection helps FedRAMP align with broader federal information security and risk

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:11.505
management practices.

00:01:11.505 --> 00:01:14.900
This course provides an educational overview of FedRAMP concepts.

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:18.875
It should not be used as professional legal, compliance, authorization, assessment,

00:01:18.875 --> 00:01:19.985
or regulatory direction.

00:01:19.985 --> 00:01:23.355
The most useful way to think about FedRAMP is as a shared trust mechanism.

00:01:23.355 --> 00:01:25.610
It gives cloud providers, federal agencies,

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:29.440
and assessors a common way to organize evidence and discuss security posture.
